Title: Restore legacy Windows support in Electron's own docs and LICENSE
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

The files Electron ships that state what it runs on, so a consumer of these
builds is not told by the package itself that its platform is unsupported.
LICENSE matters most: build.sh's package() puts it in the dist zips and --npm
copies it into the npm package, so it is the one file here that every consumer
receives.

  LICENSE                  a copyright line for the work in this repo, under
                           Electron's own. Same MIT terms; this only records
                           that the binaries contain modifications.

  README.md                Platform support said "Windows (Windows 10 and up)"
                           and listed only x64 and arm64. Restored to the
                           pre-v23 wording -- Windows 7 and up, with ia32 --
                           which is what these builds actually provide, 32-bit
                           included (see other/07-restore-32-bit.patch).

  docs/breaking-changes.md two entries describe removals this build reverses,
                           and a reader checking whether their platform works
                           would land on exactly these. Each gets a note rather
                           than an edit, so upstream's account of what upstream
                           did stays intact and only the part that differs here
                           is called out:
                             - "Removed: Windows 32-bit (ia32) and Linux 32-bit
                               ARM (armv7l) support" (44.0) -- ia32 is restored,
                               armv7l is not, so the note says so.
                             - "Removed: Windows 7 / 8 / 8.1 support" (23.0).

Deliberately left alone:

  docs/development/build-instructions-windows.md
                           the build prerequisites. Building this repo goes
                           through build.sh and its README, not Electron's
                           instructions, so patching them would be noise.

  the two `systemPreferences.isAeroGlassEnabled()` entries
                           they say Electron "only supports Windows 10+, where
                           DWM composition can no longer be disabled", whose
                           premise is wrong here -- chromium/ui/0162 restores
                           ui::win::IsAeroGlassEnabled and the non-DWM paths.
                           But the Electron API itself is not restored, so
                           annotating them would imply a replacement that does
                           not exist. The removal still stands.

